frankensim.bar-card.v1
# Marimba Gb2, on a loss law fitted through three printed rosewood bars; everything else as the C4 card, with the same certificate row.
name	marimba-gb2-fit3-rosewood-nobox-rubber
source	Bar outline: J. La Favre, lafavre.us/marimba-bars.htm, table of an unnamed commercial 5-octave marimba with rosewood bars: F# row, length 19 5/8 in; width 2 1/2 in and thickness 0.92 in from the G row. Wood: A. Chaigne and V. Doutaut, Numerical simulations of xylophones. I, J. Acoust. Soc. Am. 101(1), 539-557 (1997), Table I p.544, Rosewood bar: E 2.13e10, density 1015, eta 2.35e-7 s, gamma_B 51.58 1/s, through the packs named below (E and density are read from the pack of Doutaut and Chaigne 1994, which prints the same values). Ratios: USDA Wood Handbook FPL-GTR-282, Honduras mahogany, stand-in. Mallet: the same Table I, Korogi No. 4 rubber, K 3.7e7, 23.6 g, measured on a padouk bar; 1.0 m/s and strike 15.9 cm of 29.3 cm as the paper's Fig. 8.
note	NOT in the source: the undercut (solved here in the engine, modes only, a circular arc, half reach 249.188 mm, the blank's length limit, and middle 5.498 mm, solved alone so f1 sits at equal temperament; f2/f1 came out 3.991, untargeted, where the builder's statement for his own padouk bars is 4 from A3 to C5; the rosewood table prints no arch length and no tuning). Strike fraction carried from the xylophone bar; no marimba strike is printed. Not the recording's bar or mallet: Iowa states no bar wood and no bar outline; the recording's file names a cord mallet or a roll, and a Korogi rubber xylophone mallet is not that mallet. No resonator: a marimba has a tube under each bar, this render has none. The loss is a fit through three printed rosewood bars, two of D. latifolia and one unidentified Dalbergia from Madagascar, tried after seeing the 7.3x decay gap on A3, with no recording number in it; its span is 393 to 945 Hz, so at f1 of 92.5 Hz the loss is extrapolated. The modes are certified at a relative half-width of 1e-7, not the plan's tighter default, largest half-width 3.6e-6 Hz. Nothing above 1460 Hz is in this render: the band stays at the solve's window plan.
